Harness patch for the SetBench microbenchmarks, applied by protocol/node_build.sh
to the build COPY after build.sh has applied portability.patch. Never
applied to the vendored tree. No change touches algorithm code.

  1. Threads above 99. benchmark.cpp and benchmark_write.cpp size their
     per-thread arrays by STRESS_MAX_THREADS = 100 and number workers from 1,
     and both CBTree variants keep per-thread counters in arrays of 100, so
     any run with 100 or more threads writes out of bounds. Raised to 256
     (with MAX_THREADS_POW2 = 512 so the adapter's rngs array covers tid 256),
     which admits every thread count up to the 256 hardware threads of a dual
     EPYC 7763 with SMT on.
  2. A `seed N` argument. The fork seeds thread i's xorshift with i + 1, a
     constant, so ten repetitions replay one key set and one access sequence.
     `seed N` offsets every stream by N * 1000003; `seed 0`, or no `seed`
     argument, reproduces the original streams exactly.
  3. Zipf in the update benchmark. benchmark_write.cpp inserts about half of
     the prefill candidates and keeps only those in `important`, but sizes the
     Zipf generator to `prefill`, so ranks above important.size() index past
     the vector. The generator is now built after prefill, sized to the keys
     present. Uniform and hot-set workloads are unaffected.

The printed output format is unchanged, so the authors' parsers still work.

